UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Charging an electric car at home is one of the simplest parts of owning one, once you understand how it works. Instead of visiting a fuel station, you plug in when you arrive home and wake up to a car that is ready for the day. This guide explains the basics in plain language, so you can set up home charging safely and use it with confidence.</p><p>Home charging is convenient because it fits into your normal routine. Most daily driving uses only a small part of the battery, so an overnight charge is usually enough to cover the next day. You do not need to wait beside the car while it charges. You plug in, lock the car, and let it charge while you rest.</p><h2>How Home Charging Works</h2><p>An electric car stores energy in a large battery. A home charger sends electricity from your home supply into that battery. The car controls the charging process and stops when the battery reaches the level you have set. Many cars let you set a target charge level and a charging schedule through the dashboard or a phone app.</p><p>Charging speed depends on several things, including the type of charger, the wiring in your home, and the car itself. A standard household outlet charges slowly and is best suited to drivers who travel short distances each day. A dedicated home charger, installed on its own circuit, charges noticeably faster and is the usual choice for regular use. Your owner''s manual and the charger instructions will explain what your car supports.</p><h2>Choosing a Home Charger</h2><p>Start by thinking about how you drive. If your daily trips are short and the car can sit plugged in overnight, a simple setup may be enough. If you drive long distances, charge at irregular times, or want a tidier and more permanent installation, a dedicated wall-mounted charger is usually the better option.</p><p>Choose a charger that is made for your car and your home electrical system. Check that it is suitable for outdoor use if it will be mounted outside, and that the cable is long enough to reach the car comfortably without stretching across a walkway.</p><p>Ask a qualified electrician to check your home wiring before you buy or install anything. The electrician can confirm whether your existing supply can support a charger, whether a separate circuit is needed, and where the charger should be placed. This is not a job to do yourself unless you are qualified, because a mistake with home wiring can damage the car, the charger, or your home.</p><h2>What It Costs to Run</h2><p>Home charging is usually cheaper than using public fast chargers, because home electricity is normally priced for household use. The exact cost depends on your electricity tariff, how much you drive, and how efficiently your car uses energy. Your electricity bill will show how much you use, and many cars report how much energy each charge has added.</p><p>Some electricity tariffs offer lower rates at night. If your tariff works that way, scheduling the car to charge overnight can reduce the cost. Check the terms of your tariff first, and set the schedule in the car or charger so it charges during the cheaper hours. You do not need to stay awake to start it.</p><p>There is also a one-off cost for the charger and its installation. Prices vary by model and by the work needed in your home, so ask for a written quote from a qualified installer before you commit. The quote should cover the charger, the wiring, the installation work, and any safety devices that are required.</p><h2>Charging Safely at Home</h2><p>Safety comes first with any electrical equipment. Always follow the instructions that came with your car and your charger. Keep the charger and cable dry, clean, and free from damage. Do not use a charger with a cracked plug, a frayed cable, or signs of overheating, such as discolouration or a burning smell. If you notice any of these signs, stop using it and get it checked by a qualified person.</p><p>Do not use a long extension lead to reach the car, and do not plug a charger into a damaged or loose socket. The charger should plug directly into a suitable outlet or be wired in by an electrician. Keep the charging area clear, and store the cable neatly when it is not in use so it does not get run over or damaged.</p><p>Follow the owner''s manual for your car, and get a qualified electrician for any electrical installation or repair work. If you are unsure about anything, ask a qualified electrician or the car manufacturer before you use the charger again. It is always better to pause and ask than to risk damage or injury.</p><h2>A Simple Daily Routine</h2><p>Make charging part of your evening routine. Park, plug in, and check that charging has started. Many cars show a light or a message on the dashboard to confirm this. In the morning, unplug the cable, store it properly, and close the charging port cover before you drive away.</p><p>Home charging is at its best when it is simple, safe, and routine. Choose the right charger for your driving, have it installed by a qualified electrician, and follow the manual. Once it is set up, plugging in at home becomes as natural as charging a phone.</p>' WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>A monthly check takes only a short time, but it can prevent breakdowns, save money, and keep you safe on the road. You do not need to be a mechanic to do it. You need a few minutes, a clean cloth, and the habit of looking at your car properly once a month. Work through the list below in order, and note anything that looks wrong so you can deal with it early.</p><p>Do the checks when the car is parked on level ground and the engine is cool. Keep the owner''s manual nearby, because it shows where each part is and what level is correct for your car. If you find a problem you do not understand, book a visit to a qualified mechanic rather than guessing.</p><h2>1. Engine Oil</h2><p>Pull out the dipstick, wipe it clean, push it back in fully, and pull it out again to read the level. The oil should sit between the marks on the dipstick. If it is low, top it up with the grade of oil your owner''s manual recommends. Also look at the colour. Very dark or gritty oil may mean a service is due.</p><h2>2. Tyres and Tyre Pressure</h2><p>Check the pressure in all four tyres, including the spare if you have one. The correct pressure is listed in the owner''s manual or on a sticker inside the driver''s door. Check the tread for wear and look for cuts, bulges, or objects stuck in the rubber. Uneven wear can mean the wheels need attention.</p><h2>3. Brakes</h2><p>Listen and feel as you drive. Squealing, grinding, or a brake pedal that feels soft, spongy, or vibrates can all be signs of wear. Look through the wheels if you can see the brake pads, and check the brake fluid level under the bonnet. Do not attempt brake repairs yourself. Get a qualified mechanic to inspect the brakes if anything feels or sounds wrong.</p><h2>4. Lights</h2><p>Turn on the headlights, indicators, brake lights, reverse lights, and hazard lights, and check each one. Ask someone to stand behind the car while you press the brake pedal, or park near a wall and watch the reflection. Replace any bulb that has failed, and clean dirty lenses so the lights stay bright.</p><h2>5. Coolant</h2><p>Check the coolant level in the reservoir when the engine is cold. It should sit between the minimum and maximum marks. Never open a hot radiator cap, because hot coolant can spray out and cause serious burns. If the level keeps dropping, have the cooling system checked for a leak.</p><h2>6. Windscreen Washer Fluid</h2><p>Top up the washer fluid so you can always clean the windscreen. Check that the washer jets spray properly and that the wiper blades clear the glass without streaking, smearing, or squeaking. Replace worn wiper blades promptly, because a clear windscreen matters most in rain.</p><h2>7. Battery</h2><p>Look at the battery terminals for corrosion, which often appears as a white or green crust. Make sure the connections are tight and the battery is held firmly in place. If the engine turns over slowly when you start the car, or the lights seem dim, have the battery tested by a qualified mechanic.</p><h2>8. Power Steering Fluid</h2><p>If your car uses power steering fluid, check the level in its reservoir and look for leaks around the hoses. Low fluid can make the steering feel heavy or noisy. Many newer cars use electric power steering and have no fluid to check. Your owner''s manual will tell you which type your car has.</p><h2>9. Belts and Hoses</h2><p>Look at the belts under the bonnet for cracks, fraying, or a glazed shine. Press a hose gently. It should feel firm, not soft, swollen, or brittle. A failed belt or hose can stop the car suddenly, so report any damage to a mechanic before it breaks.</p><h2>10. Air Filter</h2><p>A dirty air filter can reduce performance and increase fuel use. Take it out and hold it up to the light. If you cannot see light through it, or it is full of dust and leaves, it probably needs cleaning or replacing. Your owner''s manual shows where it sits and how often it should be changed.</p><h2>11. Under the Car</h2><p>Look under the car for fresh puddles or drips. A small amount of clear water under the car after using the air conditioning is normal. Oil, coolant, or fuel on the ground is not normal and should be checked by a mechanic. Also look at the exhaust for rust, holes, or unusual smoke.</p><h2>12. Interior and Safety Equipment</h2><p>Check the seat belts for fraying and make sure each one clicks in and retracts properly. Test the horn, check that the mirrors are clean and correctly adjusted, and confirm that the spare tyre, jack, and warning triangle are present and in working order. A quick check of the dashboard for warning lights finishes the routine.</p><p>Write down anything you find, even if it seems small. A small leak, a worn tyre, or a weak battery is cheaper and easier to fix now than after it fails on the road. Follow the owner''s manual for your car, and get a qualified mechanic for brake work and any repair you are not sure about. A monthly check is a simple habit, and it is one of the best ways to keep your car reliable.</p>' WHERE id = 2;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Choosing between a hybrid and a petrol car is a practical decision, not just a question of fuel. A hybrid uses a petrol engine together with an electric motor and a battery, while a petrol car relies on its engine alone. Over five years, the real cost includes fuel, servicing, insurance, and what the car is worth when you sell it. This guide compares those costs honestly, so you can decide which suits your driving.</p><h2>How a Hybrid Works</h2><p>A hybrid charges its own battery while you drive, using the engine and the energy recovered when you slow down. You do not plug it in. The electric motor helps the engine at low speeds and when pulling away, which is where a petrol engine often uses the most fuel. On a steady motorway run, the difference is smaller, because the engine does most of the work in both types of car.</p><p>This means a hybrid usually saves the most fuel in town driving, with frequent stops and slow traffic. If most of your driving is short urban trips, the savings can be noticeable. If most of your driving is long motorway journeys at a steady speed, the fuel saving is likely to be modest. Think about your own journeys before you decide.</p><h2>Fuel Costs Over Five Years</h2><p>Fuel is the most obvious difference, but it is not the only one. A hybrid generally uses less fuel in town, which adds up over five years of daily driving. The size of the saving depends on how far you drive, how much of that driving is in town, and how you drive. Smooth driving with gentle acceleration helps both types of car use less fuel.</p><p>To compare fairly, work out your own annual distance and your mix of town and motorway driving. Then look at the fuel use figures for the specific models you are considering, and use the same driving pattern for both. A hybrid that saves fuel on paper may save little if your driving is mostly motorway, and a petrol car may cost more than you expect if your driving is mostly in town.</p><h2>Servicing and Maintenance</h2><p>Servicing costs are often similar for the two types, but there are differences worth knowing. A hybrid has extra parts, including a battery and an electric motor, so some repairs can be more specialised. However, the brakes on a hybrid often last longer, because the electric motor helps slow the car and reduces wear on the brake pads.</p><p>Petrol cars have a simpler drivetrain, and most mechanics are familiar with them. That can make some repairs easier to arrange. For either type, follow the service schedule in the owner''s manual and use a qualified mechanic. Skipping services to save money usually costs more later, because small problems are found too late.</p><p>Ask about the warranty on the hybrid battery before you buy. The length of cover and the terms vary by manufacturer, so read the details and keep the service history complete. A full service history also helps when you sell the car.</p><h2>Buying Price and Resale Value</h2><p>A hybrid often costs more to buy than a similar petrol car. That higher purchase price is the main reason a hybrid does not always save money overall. The fuel saving has to be large enough, over the years you keep the car, to cover the extra amount you paid at the start.</p><p>Resale value matters too. Demand for hybrids has grown as more drivers look for lower fuel costs, and that can help a hybrid hold its value. But resale values change with the market, with fuel prices, and with new models, so no one can promise what a car will be worth in five years. Treat resale as an estimate, not a guarantee.</p><p>Work out the total cost for your own situation. Add the purchase price, the fuel you expect to use, servicing, insurance, and any tax or charges that apply where you live. Then subtract a realistic resale value. Do this for both cars using the same assumptions, and the better choice for your driving will become clear.</p><h2>Which One Suits You?</h2><p>A hybrid is often the better choice if you drive mainly in town, cover a reasonable distance each year, and plan to keep the car long enough for the fuel saving to matter. A petrol car may be the better choice if you drive mainly on motorways, cover a low annual distance, or want the lowest purchase price and the simplest maintenance.</p><p>Test drive both types on roads like the ones you use every day. Notice how each car feels in traffic, how quiet it is, and how easy it is to drive. Comfort and confidence matter as much as cost, because you will live with the car every day.</p><p>There is no single right answer. A hybrid saves fuel in the right conditions, but it costs more to buy. A petrol car costs less at the start, but it may cost more in fuel. Compare the full five-year cost for your own driving, follow the owner''s manual for servicing, and choose the car that fits your journeys, your budget, and the way you drive.</p>' WHERE id = 3;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Buying your first motorcycle is exciting, and it is also a decision that deserves care. The right first bike is one you can control confidently, ride comfortably, and afford to run. It is not the fastest or the biggest bike you can buy. This guide explains how to choose well, step by step.</p><h2>Start With Your Licence and Training</h2><p>Before you look at any bike, check what your licence allows you to ride where you live. Different licences limit engine size and power, and the rules vary by country and by age. Make sure you understand the rules that apply to you before you spend any money.</p><p>Good training matters more than the bike itself. A proper training course teaches you how to control the bike, how to read the road, and how to stay safe in traffic. If you are new to riding, complete the training first and practise in a safe place before you ride on busy roads. A confident, well-trained rider is safer on a modest bike than an untrained rider on a powerful one.</p><h2>Choosing the Right Engine Size</h2><p>For a first bike, a smaller engine is usually the wiser choice. A smaller bike is lighter, easier to handle at low speed, and more forgiving when you make a mistake. It is also cheaper to buy, to insure, and to fuel. You can learn good habits on it and move to a larger bike later, when you have more experience.</p><p>A large, powerful bike can be hard to control for a new rider. It accelerates quickly, it is heavier to move at parking speed, and it can be tiring to ride. Many experienced riders recommend starting small and building up. Your first bike should help you learn, not test your limits.</p><p>Think about how you will use the bike. A small bike is ideal for town riding and short trips. If you plan regular motorway journeys, you may need something with enough power to keep up with traffic comfortably. Test ride the bikes you are considering, and notice how each one feels at the speeds you will actually ride.</p><h2>Riding Position and Comfort</h2><p>Sit on the bike before you buy it. Both feet should reach the ground comfortably, and you should be able to reach the handlebars, brakes, and clutch without stretching or straining. The seat should feel comfortable, and the riding position should feel natural, not cramped or stretched out.</p><p>There are different styles of bike, and each one suits a different kind of riding. An upright bike gives you a good view of the road and is comfortable for everyday use. A sporty bike leans you forward, which can be tiring on long rides. A cruiser sits you low with your feet forward, which suits relaxed riding. Choose the position that feels right for your body and your journeys.</p><p>Take your time. Sit on several bikes and practise pushing the bike around. A bike that feels heavy or awkward in a showroom will feel heavier on the road. Comfort and control matter more than looks.</p><h2>Safety Gear Is Not Optional</h2><p>Budget for safety gear before you buy the bike, because you will need it from your first ride. A good helmet that fits properly is the most important item. It should be comfortable, secure, and in good condition. Replace a helmet that has been dropped or involved in a crash, even if it looks fine, because hidden damage can reduce its protection.</p><p>You also need a jacket, gloves, boots, and trousers made for riding. Proper gear protects you from the weather and from injury if you fall. Ordinary clothes, trainers, and thin gloves are not enough. Buy gear that fits well and that you will actually wear on every ride, in all weathers.</p><p>Consider a bike with anti-lock brakes, often called ABS, if it is available. ABS helps prevent the wheels from locking when you brake hard, which can help you stay in control in an emergency. Ask the seller whether the bike has it, and learn how it works before you ride.</p><h2>Running Costs to Plan For</h2><p>A motorcycle can be cheap to run, but you should plan for the full cost. Fuel is usually the smallest part. Insurance for a new rider can be a large cost, so get quotes before you buy, and compare several insurers. The cost depends on the bike, your age, your experience, and where you keep the bike overnight.</p><p>Allow for servicing, tyres, and replacement parts. Tyres wear out and need replacing, and regular servicing keeps the bike safe and reliable. Follow the service schedule in the owner''s manual, and use a qualified mechanic for work you cannot do safely yourself. Keep a small reserve for unexpected repairs, especially if you buy a used bike.</p><p>If you buy used, have the bike inspected by a qualified mechanic before you pay. Check the service history, look for signs of crash damage or poor repairs, and make sure the paperwork matches the bike.</p><p>Your first bike should be one you enjoy riding and can afford to keep. Start with proper training, choose a manageable engine size, pick a riding position that fits you, buy good safety gear, and plan for the full running cost. Take your time, test ride before you buy, and follow the owner''s manual. A careful first choice makes riding safer and far more enjoyable.</p>' WHERE id = 4;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Dashboard warning lights are your car telling you that something needs attention. Some lights mean you should stop as soon as it is safe to do so. Others mean you should book a check soon. Learning what each light means helps you stay calm, make the right decision, and avoid turning a small problem into a serious one.</p><p>Warning lights use colours to show urgency. Red usually means stop and get help. Amber or yellow usually means check soon and drive with care. Green and blue usually mean a system is switched on, such as the headlights or indicators. Always check your owner''s manual for the exact meaning of each light in your car, because symbols can vary between models.</p><h2>Lights That Need Immediate Action</h2><p>The oil pressure light looks like an oil can. If it comes on while you are driving, stop as soon as it is safe, switch off the engine, and do not drive on. Low oil pressure can damage the engine very quickly. Check the oil level when the engine is cool, and call a qualified mechanic if the level is low or the light stays on.</p><p>The brake system warning light, often a circle with an exclamation mark, can mean the handbrake is on, the brake fluid is low, or there is a fault in the braking system. If the handbrake is off and the light stays on, do not keep driving. Have the brakes inspected by a qualified mechanic before you use the car again.</p><p>The battery warning light looks like a battery. It means the charging system is not working properly. The car may keep running for a short time on the battery alone, but it can stop without warning. Switch off anything you do not need, such as the heater and the radio, and drive to a safe place or a mechanic as soon as you can.</p><p>The temperature warning light, often a thermometer symbol, means the engine is overheating. Stop as soon as it is safe and switch off the engine. Wait for the engine to cool before you open the bonnet, and never open a hot radiator cap. Have the cooling system checked by a qualified mechanic before you drive again.</p><h2>Lights That Mean Check Soon</h2><p>The engine management light, often shaped like an engine, means the engine or exhaust system has detected a fault. If the light is steady and the car is driving normally, book a check with a qualified mechanic soon. If the light is flashing, or the car is losing power, shaking, or making unusual noises, stop driving and get help.</p><p>The tyre pressure light looks like a tyre with an exclamation mark. It means one or more tyres may be low on air. Check the pressure in all four tyres and inflate them to the level shown in the owner''s manual or on the driver''s door sticker. If a tyre keeps losing air, have it checked for a puncture or a damaged valve.</p><p>The ABS light means there may be a fault with the anti-lock braking system. Your normal brakes may still work, but the anti-lock function may not. Drive carefully, allow extra distance to stop, and have the system checked by a qualified mechanic soon.</p><h2>Other Common Lights</h2><p>The fuel light means the tank is low. Refuel at the next opportunity, and do not rely on the remaining fuel for a long journey. The washer fluid light means the reservoir is low. Top it up so you can always clean the windscreen.</p><p>The seat belt reminder light means a seat belt is not fastened. Fasten all seat belts before you drive. The airbag light means there may be a fault with the airbag system. Have it checked by a qualified mechanic, because the airbags may not work correctly in a crash if the light is on.</p><p>The traction control and stability lights show that those systems are working or have been switched off. If the light stays on after you start the car, or comes on while you are driving normally, check the owner''s manual and have the system inspected if needed.</p><h2>What to Do When a Light Comes On</h2><p>Stay calm and follow a simple routine. First, look at the colour of the light and think about how the car feels. Is it driving normally, or is there a noise, a smell, or a loss of power? Second, find a safe place to stop if the light is red or the car feels wrong. Third, check the owner''s manual for the exact meaning and the advice for your car.</p><p>Do not ignore a warning light and hope it goes away. A light that comes on and then goes off can still mean a fault was detected, so have it checked.</p><p>Keep a note of which light came on, when it happened, and what the car was doing at the time. That information helps a mechanic find the fault more quickly. Follow the owner''s manual for your car, and get a qualified mechanic for brake work, engine faults, and any repair you are not sure about. Learning your warning lights takes a little time, but it is one of the most useful things you can know as a driver.</p>' WHERE id = 5;
